23:38:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a perfectly logical question—and I see exactly why it feels inconsistent. Here's the straight, no-fluff distinction: **Flu viruses** are all part of the same biological family (think: all cats, from lions to housecats, still *Felidae*). They share a common ancestry, genetic structure, and life cycle. The strains are variations of that same basic organism, so "the flu virus" is one biological kind. **Cold viruses** are not. They belong to completely different families—rhinoviruses (Picornaviridae) are as different from adenoviruses (Adenoviridae) as a cat is from a frog. They have different genomes, different replication, different shapes. The only thing they have in common is that they can cause the same set of symptoms: a cold. The label "the cold virus" is based on the *illness* it causes, not the biology of the pathogen. You could equally call rhinovirus, adenovirus, and some coronaviruses all "the sore-throat-and-runny-nose virus." That's a symptom label, not a scientific grouping. In contrast, "the flu virus" is a scientific grouping that happens to cause flu. So it's not a double standard—it's that we've confused the name of the disease with the name of the pathogen. You're right to call out the apparent inconsistency; it's a result of everyday language blurring two separate categories.
